{"word": "weary", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "weary", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adjective", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈwɪə̯ɹi/", "tags": ["General Australian", "Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈwɪɹi/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈwɪɚi/", "tags": ["US"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈwiɹi/", "tags": ["US"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈwiːɹi/", "tags": ["Scotland"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈwiə̯ɹi/", "tags": ["New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈwɛːɹi/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "wearier", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "weariest", "tags": ["superlative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Having the strength exhausted by toil or exertion; tired; fatigued.", "tags": [], "examples": ["A weary traveller knocked at the door."], "quotes": [{"text": "I care not for my spirits if my legs were not weary.", "reference": "c. 1598–1600 (date written), William Shakespeare, “As You Like It”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act II, scene iv]:"}, {"text": "[I] am weary, thinking of your task.", "reference": "1863, Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, Weariness:"}, {"text": "There was a neat hat-and-umbrella stand, and the stranger's weary feet fell soft on a good, serviceable dark-red drugget, which matched in colour the flock-paper on the walls.", "reference": "1913, Mrs. [Marie] Belloc Lowndes, chapter II, in The Lodger, London: Methuen, →OCLC; republished in Novels of Mystery: The Lodger; The Story of Ivy; What Really Happened, New York, N.Y.: Longmans, Green and Co., […], [1933], →OCLC, page 0091:"}, {"text": "With the lift in the block still out of order, they climbed the flights and flights of steps. When Dad finally put the key in the front door, both were weary beyond words.", "reference": "2017, David Walliams [pseudonym; David